From 066a5ab07103558ebe9b3c98d449ef80b67c8680 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 17:39:28 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] feat(chexc): annotate the fs/io checked flows (proposal
 5.3, phase 2)

90 'throws' clauses across std.fs and std.io -- the half of the warn-mode
enumeration that the classification principle (I/O, parse, net,
concurrency-wait -> checked) decides without judgment.

Derived mechanically in four rings, each read off the previous warn-mode
build: ring 1 the direct throwers (45), ring 2 their Path/String overload
twins (31), ring 3 the writeln family (13), ring 4 one straggler. No design
input at any ring -- which is the argument for the --suggest-throws fixer
sketched in notes/production-gap-analysis.md: this entire diff could have
been generated.

Converged to 8 remaining warnings, all of them blocked by an interface
contract rather than by judgment: File.read/write/seek/close (IOStream,
Seekable, Resource), ByteBuffer.seek, ChainedInputStream.read, and
StringReader's two next(). Annotating an implementation whose interface
method has no clause violates 3.8, so the clause belongs on the interface --
the single highest-leverage decision in the migration, costed in
notes/stdlib-migration-questions.md as Q-STD-5, with Iterator.next() (Q-STD-6)
the sharper case: no iterator can do checked-throwing work at all until the
protocol takes capability polymorphism. Both left warning, deliberately.

Everything else stays a warning pending rulings (Q-STD-1..7 and the 23
UNCERTAIN inventory classes), per the directive to annotate only the obvious.

Verified: std builds clean; CheckedExceptions + API/std/{core,fs,io,mock}
466/466.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
